The problem with English is that the "not" in "shouldn't have" and "mustn't
go" should normally be parsed as "should not-have" and "must not-go", but
are in fact usually "should-not have" and "must-not go". Only emphatic
intonation and pauses get this idea across, and thus it rarely appears in
writing.
In Esperanto, e.g., it's obvious which is meant: "ne devas iri" vs. "devas
ne iri"; Russian (i.a.) shares this, I believe.

stevo


